        Theoretical Foundation: Periodic Trends in Ionic Charges, Thermodynamic &amp; Electronic Properties
      </h2>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Understanding element charges and oxidation states is the cornerstone of chemical nomenclature, stoichiometry, and industrial synthesis. In an ideal chemical system, atoms combine by transferring or sharing valence electrons to attain a stable, low-energy noble gas electron configuration—commonly referred to as the octet rule. However, real-world chemical bonding is nuanced, governed by periodic trends such as electronegativity, atomic radius, ionization energy, and electron affinity.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        When designing precursor solutions or studying materials like metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and nanostructured oxides, researchers must evaluate both fixed ionic charges (typical of s-block alkali and alkaline earth metals) and variable oxidation states (characteristic of d-block transition metals and p-block heavier elements). This interactive periodic table is engineered to bridge raw atomic data with practical laboratory workflows, allowing students and investigators to inspect charge states and transition directly into solution preparation calculations.
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; margin: 2rem 0;">
        <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.5rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; margin-top: 0; color: #0284c7;">Main Group Elements (s and p Blocks)</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            Main group elements exhibit predictable, systematic ionic charges dictated by their group valence. Group 1 elements (alkali metals like lithium and sodium) reliably lose one valence electron to form stable +1 cations. Group 2 elements form +2 cations. Nonmetals in Groups 15, 16, and 17 gain electrons to achieve noble gas configurations, yielding stable -3, -2, and -1 anions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.5rem;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; margin-top: 0; color: #059669;">Transition Metals (d Block)</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            Transition metals frequently display multiple stable oxidation states. Elements such as iron (Fe<sup>2+</sup>, Fe<sup>3+</sup>), copper (Cu<sup>+</sup>, Cu<sup>2+</sup>), and manganese (+2, +4, +7) utilize d-orbital electrons alongside s-electrons during bonding, adjusting their valency depending on the thermodynamic environment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

